  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-97004",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =7x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
